<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Etna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aetna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Etna is an active volcano on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and the highest mountain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, about 3,300 metres high. Its fertile lower slopes attracted Greek colonists, who founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catana</a> at its foot in 729 BCE, and its eruptions repeatedly damaged the city. Greek writers placed the forge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephaestus">Hephaestus</a> and the buried giant Typhon beneath it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-etna.jpg" alt="Mount Etna" width="320" height="130" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BenAveling · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mt_Etna_and_Catania1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Etna is a stratovolcano in the Metropolitan City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catania</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> and Catania, above the convergent boundary of the African and Eurasian plates. It covers 1,190 square kilometres, with a base 140 kilometres around, and is about two and a half times the height of Vesuvius, the next largest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s four active volcanoes. Its fertile volcanic soils carry vineyards and orchards on the lower slopes and the Plain of Catania. Most eruptions come from five summit craters, while the flanks hold more than 300 vents.</p><p>Volcanic activity began about 500,000 years ago under the sea off the ancient Sicilian coast. A catastrophic collapse of the eastern flank, thought to have happened around 8,000 years ago, left the Valle del Bove and may have caused a tsunami in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. The mountain is moving towards the sea at an average of 14 millimetres a year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first recorded eruption is reported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> at about 1400 BCE. An eruption in 396 BCE reportedly stopped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> advancing on Syracuse. A violent summit eruption in 122 BCE brought tephra that collapsed roofs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catania</a>, and the Roman government exempted its people from taxes for ten years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> probably described an eruption at first hand in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a>. The name may derive from the Greek verb meaning to burn, or from a Phoenician word for furnace. Greek and Roman myth placed the forge of Vulcan, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephaestus">Hephaestus</a>, beneath it and said that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> trapped the monster Typhon under the mountain.</p><p>The mountain was also called Mongibello, a name tied to Arthurian romance, in which it became the otherworld realm of Morgan le Fay and Arthur. The eruption that began on 11 March 1669 destroyed at least ten villages and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catania</a>'s walls on 15 April, filling its harbour. Reports of up to 15,000 dead are not supported by contemporary accounts, which mention no deaths, and only 77 deaths are attributed with certainty to Etna's eruptions. In 1928 lava almost completely destroyed Mascali, and the regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> used the rebuilding for propaganda. Operation Hot Rock diverted lava from Zafferana Etnea in 1992.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Etna remains one of the world's most active volcanoes and is almost constantly active. It has been designated a Decade Volcano by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> and was added to the UNESCO World Heritage list in June 2013. The Circumetnea railway, built between 1889 and 1895, runs 110 kilometres round it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catania</a> to Riposto, and the Sapienza Refuge on the south side is the base for a cableway and ski slopes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mount Etna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chalcidian colonists from Naxos founded Catana in 729 BCE, led by Euarchus, on the coast south of <b>Mount Etna</b>, in a fertile plain watered by the Simeto.</blockquote><a href="/state/catana">Catana</a></li><li><blockquote>It grew vines on the slopes of <b>Etna</b>, and its coins with Dionysus and a satyr are celebrated.</blockquote><a href="/state/naxos-sicily">Naxos (Sicily)</a></li><li><blockquote>Its theatre, built in the Hellenistic period and enlarged by the Romans, overlooks the coast and <b>Mount Etna</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/tauromenium">Tauromenium</a></li></ul></section>
